    schemeCitation: "Marking points quoted from the SEC marking scheme, Construction Studies 2021 Ordinary Level — © State Examinations Commission.",
    id: "cons-2021-ol-q8-None-joist-bridging", topicId: "cons-4-3", conceptId: "construction-terms-joist-bridging",
    section: "B", questionRef: "2021 OL Q8",
    questionText: "Explain, with the aid of notes and freehand sketches, any five of the following: joist bridging; steel reinforcement; biscuit joint; working drawing; heat pump; room thermostat; rooflight; ridgeboard; building energy rating.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"fixed"}, totalMarks: 50,
    rows: [
    { id: "terms", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "Construction Terms", marks: 50, contextNote: "Explain any five of the nine terms. Each term is marked separately: primary communication of relevant information 6 and other communication 4, in notes or sketches (the scheme reserves no fixed split between them). Joist bridging: keeps floor joists vertical and stops them twisting; stiffens and ties the joists together and spreads load to neighbouring joists; solid or herringbone bridging. Steel reinforcement: combined with concrete as reinforced concrete, taking the tension in load-bearing members; placed low in a foundation; patterned bars (rebar) or mesh for grip, tied into cages. Biscuit joint: used in modern furniture for butt and edge joints in boards of different widths and thicknesses; pressed beech biscuits fit grooves cut by a biscuit joiner, giving a larger gluing area. Working drawing: a scaled drawing used as a guide when building furniture or buildings, with elevations, plan and sections, dimensions and notes; hand drafted or CAD; often called plans or blueprints. Heat pump: moves heat from one place to another, such as from the outside air or ground into the house for heating and hot water, very efficiently. Room thermostat: senses the room temperature and switches the heating to keep it at the chosen level; dial, digital or smart (Wi-Fi) control; lower heating bills and less waste. Roof light: a window in the roof surface for natural light, double, triple or quadruple glazed; lets an attic become a usable room; more privacy than a dormer. Ridge board: the horizontal member at the top ends of the rafters forming the apex of a cut roof; rafters are nailed to it; supported by the rafters or gables; typically 225 × 32 or 225 × 25 mm. Building Energy Rating: a certificate rating a home’s energy performance from A to G, calculated on the energy for space heating, hot water, ventilation and lighting; A is the most efficient with the lowest bills.", group: {"claimMax":5,"perOption":10,"options":["Joist bridging","Steel reinforcement","Biscuit joint","Working drawing","Heat pump","Room thermostat","Roof light","Ridge board","Building Energy Rating"]} },
    ],
