    schemeCitation: "Marking points quoted from the SEC marking scheme, Construction Studies 2023 Ordinary Level — © State Examinations Commission.",
    id: "cons-2023-ol-q8-None-concrete-mixer", topicId: "cons-2-7", conceptId: "construction-terms-concrete-mixer",
    section: "B", questionRef: "2023 OL Q8",
    questionText: "Explain, with the aid of notes and freehand sketches, any five of the following: concrete mixer; smart radiator valve; ridge tile; triple glazing; balusters; cedar cladding; bridle joint; wood pellet stove; floor screed.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"fixed"}, totalMarks: 50,
    rows: [
    { id: "terms", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "Construction Terms", marks: 50, contextNote: "Explain any five of the nine terms. Each term is marked separately: primary communication of relevant information 6 and other communication 4, in notes or sketches (the scheme reserves no fixed split between them). Concrete mixer: a rotating drum that turns raw materials into concrete without segregation; small on-site batch mixers (electric, petrol or diesel) are hand loaded and make up to 200 litres per batch, so quality control is difficult; larger quantities come from a mixing plant in a ready-mix truck of 4–6 cubic metres, better regulated and poured directly into the work area. Smart radiator valve: fitted to new or existing radiators; integrated sensors measure the room temperature and signal to reduce or increase the flow of hot water; controlled from smart devices when the owners are away; each room can have its own temperature; reduces energy cost and use. Ridge tile: sits on the apex of the roof and seals the intersection of the two roof planes; traditionally bedded in mortar, now often fixed with integrated dry-fix systems; made from materials like the roof covering, in a variety of colours and profiles. Triple glazing: three panes of glass with cavities usually filled with an inert gas (argon, xenon or krypton); reduces noise and insulates better than double glazing; heavier and thicker, so it may not suit all frames; it can reduce solar gain, so some owners avoid it on the southern elevation. Balusters: vertical members between the handrail and string of a stairs, also called spindles; moulded, square or lathe-turned; must not let a 100 mm diameter sphere pass between them; made of wood, iron or plastic. Cedar cladding: naturally durable, lasting over 60 years without preservative treatment, which is only needed to avoid the weathered silver-grey colour; fixed horizontally or vertically to battens with stainless steel or galvanised fixings, as cedar reacts with ferrous metals; low embodied energy. Bridle joint: a common, strong woodworking joint with a large gluing area; a tee or corner bridle, removing one third of the material from one piece and two thirds from the other; used in frame construction and to join table rails to uprights. Wood pellet stove: heats a home by burning renewable pellets of compressed sawdust and waste wood; an auger feeds fuel steadily from a hopper for a constant flame needing little adjustment; a low-cost heating alternative with very low emissions and a smaller environmental impact. Floor screed: the final layer of the floor on which a floor finish is laid; traditionally sand and cement at 3:1 to 5:1, levelled with a tamping board; modern free-flowing calcium sulphate screeds self-level and suit underfloor heating, at 50–75 mm over pipes clipped to the rigid insulation.", group: {"claimMax":5,"perOption":10,"options":["Concrete mixer","Smart radiator valve","Ridge tile","Triple glazing","Balusters","Cedar cladding","Bridle joint","Wood pellet stove","Floor screed"]} },
    ],
