    schemeCitation: "Marking points quoted from the SEC marking scheme, Construction Studies 2024 Ordinary Level — © State Examinations Commission.",
    id: "cons-2024-ol-q8-None-site-planning-notice", topicId: "cons-1-3", conceptId: "construction-terms-site-planning-notice",
    section: "B", questionRef: "2024 OL Q8",
    questionText: "Explain, with the aid of notes and freehand sketches, any five of the following: site planning notice; halving joint; faceplate turning; smart lighting; cordless drill; plywood; gulley trap; cavity closer; heat pump.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"fixed"}, totalMarks: 50,
    rows: [
    { id: "terms", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "Construction Terms", marks: 50, contextNote: "Explain any five of the nine terms. Each term is marked separately: primary communication of relevant information 6 and other communication 4, in notes or sketches (the scheme reserves no fixed split between them). Site planning notice: a public notice the applicant erects at the site when applying for planning permission, clearly visible and legible from the road, giving the applicant’s name, site address, type of application and nature of the development; required by planning regulations so the public can make observations or objections; in place for the first five weeks after lodgement, or the application may be invalid. Halving joint: a common, strong woodworking joint with a medium gluing area, made by removing half the material from each piece at the joint; cross, corner, T- or dovetail halving; used in framing and furniture. Faceplate turning: a woodturning method for large, flat circular items such as bowls and plates; the work piece is mounted on a faceplate on the headstock of the lathe; the tool rest supports the chisel; start slowly until the piece is balanced, then increase the speed. Smart lighting: lighting controlled remotely, often automatically, through a smartphone, tablet or voice commands over the internet; scheduling and timers; customised colour, brightness and ambiance; contributes to energy savings. Cordless drill: a portable power tool for drilling holes and driving screws, powered by rechargeable (usually lithium-ion) batteries; more convenient and versatile than a corded drill and usable where there is no power source; features such as an LED light and battery indicator. Plywood: an engineered panel of thin wood veneers glued with the grain of each layer perpendicular to the next for strength; used in construction, furniture and cabinet-making; a strong, relatively low-cost alternative to solid wood; marine plywood for high-moisture use. Gulley trap: a fitting in foul and rainwater drainage with a U-bend that holds water as a seal against sewer gases and odours entering the building; typically underground; needs regular maintenance. Cavity closer: closes the gap between the leaves of a cavity wall at wallplate level and around window and door openings; prevents thermal bridging, damp penetration, air infiltration and condensation; insulating foam or rigid board, 100–150 mm wide to suit the cavity. Heat pump: a device transferring heat from the air, water or ground into the home, distributed by underfloor heating or radiators; runs on electricity with a coefficient of performance of about 3–4; grants encourage the change from fossil-fuel heating.", group: {"claimMax":5,"perOption":10,"options":["Site planning notice","Halving joint","Faceplate turning","Smart lighting","Cordless drill","Plywood","Gulley trap","Cavity closer","Heat pump"]} },
    ],
