    schemeCitation: "Marking points quoted from the SEC marking scheme, Construction Studies 2018 Ordinary Level — © State Examinations Commission.",
    id: "cons-2018-ol-q8-terms", topicId: "cons-5-8", conceptId: "construction-terms-terms",
    section: "B", questionRef: "2018 OL Q8",
    questionText: "Explain, with the aid of notes and freehand sketches, any five of the following: smart meter; biscuit joint; earth rod; airtightness tape; evacuated tube; magnetic catch; roof-light window; aerated shower; LED lighting.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"fixed"}, totalMarks: 50,
    rows: [
    { id: "terms", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "Terms", marks: 50, contextNote: "Explain any five of the nine terms. Each term is marked separately: primary communication of relevant information 6 and other communication 4, in notes or sketches (the scheme reserves no fixed split between them). Smart meter: records the electricity used in the home and shows its cost, giving awareness and control of energy use; it can send readings to the supplier and may also record water or gas. Biscuit joint: joins boards of different widths in modern furniture; a biscuit joiner cuts a groove in each edge, and glued pressed-beech biscuits are inserted before the boards are clamped. Earth rod: a copper or steel rod, usually 1200 mm long, driven outside the building and connected to the electrical system; it carries fault current and lightning to earth, preventing shock and fire. Airtightness tape: tape sealing joints in the structure — around windows, doors, floors, ceilings, joists, pipes and ducts — to prevent draughts and reduce heat loss. Evacuated tube: part of a solar panel: a vacuum glass tube around a copper heat pipe whose glycol fluid vaporises, rises to a header and heats the coil in the hot water cylinder. Magnetic catch: a catch for cabinet and wardrobe doors: a base unit with magnetised strips on the frame and a striking plate on the door, carefully aligned so they meet when the door closes. Roof light window: an opening window for sloping, flat or low-pitched roofs, double, triple or quadruple glazed, letting light into rooms in an attic development. Aerated shower: mixes air with the flow of water, cutting use to about 7 litres a minute against 12 for a traditional shower while keeping a comfortable flow; it saves water and energy. LED lighting: light-emitting diodes for domestic and commercial fittings, using about 90% less energy than halogen bulbs and lasting far longer, though dearer to buy.", group: {"claimMax":5,"perOption":10,"options":["Smart meter","Biscuit joint","Earth rod","Airtightness tape","Evacuated tube","Magnetic catch","Roof light window","Aerated shower","LED lighting"]} },
    ],
